# Appendix D - API, data handling, and security (9 pages)
add("D.1 Data journey and processing boundaries", "The email text begins in a React field or is read from the Gmail page. The client sends it with a tone to the local backend. The backend composes a prompt and sends that prompt to the configured Gemini endpoint. The model response is parsed and returned to the client, where it is rendered or inserted into a draft. This flow crosses the browser, backend, and provider trust boundaries.", ["The repository shows no database writes for message content.","The absence of storage code is not a proof that no operational logs or provider retention exist.","The user must review the provider and deployment data policies before using sensitive email."], "A data-flow diagram for a real deployment should identify collection, transit encryption, processing, logging, retention, access roles, and deletion practices.")
add("D.2 Data minimization and purpose limitation", "A reply-generation request needs the content required to draft a response, but not necessarily an entire thread or unrelated signature history. The current client reads a selector's innerText and can therefore capture more context than intended, depending on Gmail's DOM. The standalone UI sends everything pasted by the user. A safer design makes the transmitted scope visible and gives the user a chance to remove irrelevant sections.", ["Limit the selected message to the active conversation content needed for the reply.","Strip unnecessary quoted history only when doing so does not remove essential context.","Avoid collecting contact lists, attachments, or mailbox metadata for a text-only reply feature."], "Minimization should be tested with long threads, quoted replies, signatures, and forwarded messages to ensure the user can understand what leaves the client.")
add("D.3 Threat model and trust boundaries", "The main assets are the provider credential, email text, generated response, user session, backend availability, and extension permissions. Relevant threats include credential disclosure, unauthorized API use, prompt injection embedded in a message, cross-origin abuse, denial of service, accidental transmission of private content, and malicious or incorrect generated wording. The supplied prototype has no formal threat model artifact.", ["Treat email text as untrusted input even when it is displayed in a trusted mailbox.","Treat model output as untrusted content and never execute it as HTML or code.","Constrain who can call the backend and how much work each request can trigger."], "A security review should rank threats by likelihood and impact and link each mitigation to a test case and deployment control.")
add("D.4 Authentication, authorization, and quotas", "No user authentication or authorization layer is present in the controller. With CORS set to all origins, any browser origin able to reach the endpoint may attempt a request, subject to network controls. A publicly accessible service would need a deliberate access model, quota enforcement, and abuse protections so that an exposed route cannot be used to spend provider quota or process unwanted content.", ["Choose whether the service is single-user, organization-restricted, or public before deployment.","Apply per-user or per-client rate controls and request size limits.","Protect any user identity token and avoid treating CORS as authentication."], "Authorization should be applied by the backend and verified with negative tests for missing, invalid, and insufficient credentials.")
add("D.5 Input validation and resource limits", "The current web form rejects a blank message, but a caller can bypass that UI and post directly to the controller. The DTO does not declare length or content validation. Extremely long input can increase provider cost and latency; malformed JSON or missing fields can produce failures that are not converted to a clear client response. Validation is needed before prompt construction.", ["Require non-empty emailContent and enforce a documented maximum size.","Accept only recognized tone values or normalize unknown values safely.","Set request timeouts and guard against repeated generation requests."], "Validation rules should be shared as contract documentation but enforced on the server. Return field-specific messages without echoing private email text.")
add("D.6 API key and credential handling", "The checked-in properties use environment placeholders instead of hard-coded key values. The service then appends the key to the configured URL. URL-based secrets can be captured by proxies, diagnostics, browser or server logs, and monitoring systems. The provider's current authentication documentation should guide a secure request header and key scope. The key belongs only in the server environment or secret store.", ["Do not place credentials in frontend build variables, extension files, screenshots, or sample requests.","Use a restricted key with rotation and monitoring where the provider supports it.","Redact authorization data from request logs and exception output."], "Credential handling should be validated with a log review and a configuration audit before release. Rotate the key if it is accidentally committed or exposed.")
add("D.7 Network transport, CORS, and extension origins", "The current clients are configured to call localhost over HTTP. This is convenient for local development, but non-local traffic should use HTTPS. The backend allows every origin through a wildcard CORS annotation; this should be replaced with an explicit policy that reflects the deployed React origin and extension context. The manifest declares the host access needed for Gmail and the local endpoint.", ["Use HTTPS between browser, backend, and provider for deployed environments.","Keep local development endpoints separate from production configuration.","Test preflight and extension-origin behavior after narrowing the CORS policy."], "A deployment checklist should include certificate validity, allowed origins, extension host permissions, and protection against mixed-content failures.")
add("D.8 Prompt injection and generated content", "A source email may itself contain instructions that are irrelevant to the requested reply or that attempt to manipulate the model. The current prompt concatenates email content after an instruction without a clear trust-boundary statement. A defensive prompt can instruct the model to treat the email only as context, avoid following embedded commands, and refrain from inventing facts. Prompt wording alone is not a complete security control.", ["Keep the source email clearly delimited from developer instructions.","Ask for a concise draft and placeholders where information is missing.","Require user review before external use and provide an easy discard/edit path."], "Measure refusal, omission, hallucination, and tone behavior using a curated non-sensitive test set. Do not characterize a single example as a safety guarantee.")
add("D.9 Logging, privacy notices, and retention", "The inspected application does not add a persistence layer for generated replies, but the report cannot infer provider retention settings or hosting logs from the source files alone. A production operator should define what is logged, who can read it, and how long it is retained. The user should receive a clear notice before sending email text to the model service.", ["Prefer metadata such as request status and latency over content-level logs.","Set retention and access controls for operational logs and rotate logs on schedule.","Document provider processing, retention, and data residency relevant to the deployment."], "A privacy review should be completed against the actual deployment, not solely the source repository, and should be revisited when the provider or hosting arrangement changes.")

add("D.10 Request and response contract review", "The clients and backend currently exchange a compact JSON request containing emailContent and tone, while the backend returns a raw string. That minimal contract is easy to begin with, yet its null handling, length limits, tone vocabulary, and error representation are not explicit. A documented contract can keep the web interface and extension aligned as the prototype evolves.", ["Define required and optional fields, accepted tone values, encoding, and maximum body size.","Distinguish successful reply text from validation, provider, and parsing failures.","Version any incompatible contract change and update both clients together."], "Treat this as a proposed contract refinement. The current source remains the authority for implemented behavior until the changes are made and verified.")

# Appendix E - extended validation plan (12 pages)
add("E.1 Validation strategy and evidence rules", "Validation should progress from deterministic service tests to API integration, browser behavior, and user evaluation. The model provider should be mocked for tests that verify request shape and response parsing; a small number of live requests can separately measure current integration behavior using non-sensitive content. This report records proposed checks only. It does not claim any listed test has been executed.", ["Record the build revision, runtime versions, configuration category, browser version, and test date.","Use fictional or consented test content with no personal identifiers.","Attach screenshots or logs only after removing keys, addresses, and private messages."], "A test result should contain the observed outcome and evidence reference, not merely a planned expected result.")
add("E.2 API contract tests", "Controller tests should establish the request and response contract independently from the model provider. A mocked service can confirm that valid values are bound and delegated. Once validation is added, tests should cover missing fields, whitespace, unsupported tones, oversized content, malformed JSON, and consistent status codes. These checks prevent the browser UI from being the only gatekeeper.", ["Valid request returns the documented success schema.","Invalid request returns a client error without invoking the provider.","Unexpected service failure returns a stable error identifier rather than raw exception text."], "Capture request/response examples in the API reference and ensure the frontend handles both success and failure models.", [["Case","Input condition","Expected behavior"],["API-01","Valid message and tone","Success response with non-empty draft"],["API-02","Missing emailContent","Validation error"],["API-03","Unknown tone value","Reject or normalize per contract"],["API-04","Payload over size limit","Reject before provider call"]])
add("E.3 Prompt construction tests", "Prompt tests should verify how the service combines the base instruction, tone selection, delimiters, and message content. The current default professional wording should be corrected before evaluating Casual and Friendly. Tests can assert structural properties without requiring a live model, including that a missing tone does not add an empty instruction and that the email appears in the designated context section.", ["Check every supported tone and the no-tone path.","Check quotes, Unicode characters, line breaks, and long message snippets.","Confirm that prompt wording does not ask the model to create a subject line."], "Prompt tests should be updated whenever instruction wording, model endpoint, or response format changes.")
add("E.4 Provider response parsing tests", "The response parser should be tested against valid output, empty candidate arrays, missing content, missing parts, refusal or blocked responses, malformed JSON, and provider error payloads. The current parser selects candidates[0] and parts[0], so negative cases are especially important. A stable error model lets the client distinguish provider failure from an empty reply.", ["Test one valid candidate with one text part.","Test absent and null candidate structures without uncaught exceptions.","Test upstream HTTP error bodies without returning raw secrets or stack traces."], "Add timeouts and a bounded retry policy only after considering duplicate requests and cost. Store sanitized diagnostics for support.")
add("E.5 React input and form behavior", "The UI validation plan covers keyboard use, multiline text entry, tone selection, disabled state, and request lifecycle. At present, the Generate Reply control is disabled for an empty trimmed input, and a blank submit handler also sets a validation message. Test the actual behavior at narrow and wide viewports, including focus order and assistive technology labels.", ["Check empty, whitespace-only, normal, and very long input.","Check that the tone control selection is included in the request.","Check button disablement during loading and recovery after success or failure."], "A visual screenshot is evidence of appearance only; it does not prove that form controls or accessibility behavior work correctly.")
add("E.6 React output, copy, and failure behavior", "The result panel should handle an empty server reply, multiline text, long output, and non-string response data. Clipboard permission may be unavailable, so the UI should explain success or offer a selectable fallback. Failure messaging should be actionable without leaking private email text or service secrets. The current implementation shows a generic retry message and does not report copy status.", ["Check newline preservation and long response wrapping.","Check clipboard success and failure paths.","Check that a new request clears stale errors and stale output when appropriate."], "Accessibility checks should verify that loading and error state changes are announced and that focus remains predictable.")
add("E.7 Gmail extension injection tests", "The extension should be tested against a controlled mailbox with one message, multiple messages, new compose windows, replies, forwarded messages, and absent toolbar elements. The MutationObserver should add only one action per relevant compose surface. Selector fallback loops should be corrected so later candidates are evaluated rather than returning on the first miss.", ["Open and close compose windows repeatedly and count injected controls.","Test multiple simultaneous compose windows without cross-linking message text.","Test navigation between conversations and verify the captured content belongs to the active one."], "Record Chrome version and Gmail layout date because DOM changes may alter integration behavior without source changes.")
add("E.8 Gmail content extraction and insertion tests", "Extraction tests should confirm exactly which visible message text is sent, including quoted history and signatures. Insertion tests should confirm the response appears at the intended cursor position in the active draft and does not overwrite existing text. The current code uses document.execCommand('insertText'), which may behave differently as browser support evolves.", ["Confirm the extracted text is not empty when a supported message element exists.","Confirm insertion does not replace the user's existing draft or send the message.","Verify failure to find the compose box produces a visible recovery option."], "Prefer supported editor or mail APIs if available, and retain a copy/paste fallback for DOM integration failures.")
add("E.9 Network and resilience tests", "The clients should be checked under backend unavailability, slow responses, abrupt disconnects, invalid JSON, and model quota errors. The React spinner should eventually stop, the user should see a safe error, and the backend should not leave worker threads blocked indefinitely. The extension should restore button text and disabled state in all outcomes, which its finally block currently attempts to do.", ["Test connection refused and timeout conditions.","Test server 4xx and 5xx responses.","Test repeated clicks and duplicate in-flight requests."], "Use explicit timeout budgets and monitor latency distributions before tuning retries. Avoid automatic retries that can multiply provider usage unexpectedly.")
add("E.10 Security and privacy validation", "Security tests should verify that no credential is present in frontend assets, browser network URLs, source control, logs, or screenshots. Confirm that only authorized origins can call the API, requests are encrypted in deployment, payload limits are enforced, and the privacy notice accurately describes provider processing. Test prompt injection with synthetic content only.", ["Inspect built JavaScript and extension package for secrets.","Review redacted server logs after success and failure cases.","Check access-control and rate-limit behavior from an untrusted origin."], "Use a qualified security review for production deployment; this report is not a penetration test or privacy certification.")
add("E.11 Usability and quality evaluation", "A user study could compare the time and effort to draft a reply manually with the time to review and revise an AI proposal. Participants should use representative, non-sensitive tasks and rate relevance, tone, correctness, edit effort, and trust. The report should state the participant count, recruitment, task order, and limitations rather than presenting anecdotes as generalizable results.", ["Track completion time and number of edits without collecting actual private mail.","Ask whether the user understood that the text was model-generated.","Record unsupported facts or tone mismatches as distinct error categories."], "Any conclusion about productivity improvement requires measured data and an appropriate baseline; no such measurement was supplied for this report.")
add("E.12 Results log template and acceptance gate", "Use the following result sheet when the proposed cases are run. Acceptance should require evidence for the happy path, validation, upstream failure, extension recovery, and privacy controls. A case marked blocked or not run should remain visible in the final submission rather than being silently counted as passed.", ["Record case ID, environment, actual result, pass/fail/blocked state, evidence file, and defect ID.","Re-run affected cases after a fix and preserve the earlier result for traceability.","Review unresolved high-impact defects before any public or institutional demonstration."], "This report's matrix remains a plan. Fill in the actual observations only after running the relevant application and retain the evidence securely.", [["Case ID","Observed result","Status","Evidence / defect"],["________","________________","________","________________"],["________","________________","________","________________"],["________","________________","________","________________"]])

# Appendix F - maintenance, release, and handover (6 pages)
add("F.1 Change management and version traceability", "The repository's Git history can provide the authoritative code revision for a release, but no milestone or change log was included in the supplied report materials. A maintainable project should associate each report or demonstration build with a commit identifier, dependency lockfiles, and a brief release note. Changes to model prompts and APIs deserve explicit tracking because they can alter generated behavior without changing the visible interface.", ["Record the source revision and build date for every demonstration.","Review package updates and backend dependency updates deliberately.","Note prompt and provider-model changes in release documentation."], "Keep reports and screenshots linked to the source revision they describe. Do not reuse an old screenshot to represent materially changed behavior.")
add("F.2 Build and deployment packaging", "The web interface can be built by Vite, the backend by Maven, and the Chrome extension from its manifest and content files. The current README references folders and a React extension structure that do not exactly match the source tree. Packaging instructions should be corrected, and development URLs should be replaced with deployment configuration. Secrets and user data must remain outside public build assets.", ["Build each component from its actual directory.","Pin or review dependency versions and archive the generated build outputs.","Verify the extension manifest's host permissions before packaging."], "A release package should include configuration examples with placeholders only, version identifiers, and a concise installation and rollback procedure.")
add("F.3 Operational monitoring and support", "A production service needs availability, latency, and error monitoring to distinguish client failures from provider failures. Metrics should avoid message content and credentials. Health endpoints, request correlation identifiers, and sanitized error categories can improve diagnosis. The current source includes Actuator as a declared dependency in the README but the checked pom.xml dependencies should be reconciled before relying on an Actuator endpoint.", ["Track request count, latency, upstream status category, and error rate.","Set alert thresholds and define an owner for provider credential rotation.","Keep logs access-controlled and retain them only as long as needed."], "Monitoring should not turn a privacy-sensitive drafting service into a repository of the user's email content.")
add("F.4 Accessibility and inclusive interaction", "The React interface uses Material UI form controls with visible labels and status elements, but accessibility must be verified in an actual browser. Check keyboard navigation, visible focus, screen-reader names, error announcement, contrast, and zoom. Gmail injection should use a control with an accessible name and a clear progress state. The current DOM button is built as a div with role=button, so keyboard support needs review.", ["Confirm all actions work by keyboard alone.","Announce loading, success, and errors to assistive technology.","Provide an accessible name and keyboard activation for the Gmail action."], "Accessibility findings should be recorded as implementation issues and rechecked after each UI change.")
add("F.5 Roadmap and release phases", "A reasonable roadmap begins with correctness and reliability, then privacy and deployment, followed by usability research and optional product features. Fix the prompt and response parser before adding additional tones. Add server-side validation, controlled CORS, HTTPS, timeouts, and error mapping before a broader deployment. Only after these steps should the project assess language selection, user preferences, or mailbox-provider expansion.", ["Phase 1: stabilize request validation and response behavior.","Phase 2: harden credentials, transport, access controls, and privacy messaging.","Phase 3: test and improve Gmail integration; measure usability with consented users."], "Each phase should have acceptance criteria, an owner, and evidence. Avoid describing roadmap items as present capabilities.")
add("F.6 Submission and handover checklist", "Before institutional submission, complete academic details on the bonafide certificate and attach the approved similarity report. Confirm that the report's author and project title are correct and that the two screenshots match the described workflow states. Execute the planned validation cases and update Chapter 4 with the real outcomes rather than the current 'not executed' status.", ["Fill supervisor, enrollment, examiner, and submission date details using verified information.","Keep the supplied app and Gmail screenshots tied to the described workflow states.","Attach the similarity report and reconcile references, page numbers, and release revision."], "Two supplied screenshots are included: the web application initial state and the Gmail compose view with the AI Reply control. Neither image demonstrates a live Gemini generation or verified draft insertion.")

# Additional project specifications, interaction scenarios, test evidence forms, and release notes.
extra_sections = [
("G", "PROJECT REQUIREMENTS AND DESIGN RECORD", [
("Scope and actors", "SmartMail Assistant has two client entry points: a standalone React page and a Chrome content script intended for Gmail. The person composing the message is the primary actor; the backend and Gemini endpoint are supporting services. The report treats the system as a draft assistant, not an autonomous mail agent.", ["The user supplies source text and may select a tone in the web client.","The backend prepares the request and returns generated text.","The user remains responsible for reviewing and deciding how to use the draft."], "Any feature outside these boundaries should be recorded as future work until it is present and verified."),
("Functional requirement map", "Requirements can be traced from visible controls through the API to the provider. This map helps reviewers separate a feature request from evidence that the supplied source implements it.", ["FR-01: accept non-empty email text in the React client.","FR-02: submit email text and tone to the configured API.","FR-03: display a returned reply and provide a copy action.","FR-04: expose an AI Reply action in Gmail and attempt draft insertion."], "Trace each requirement to a source file and a test case; mark evidence as source-inspected or executed."),
("Quality attribute priorities", "The prototype's most important quality attributes are correctness of the draft workflow, privacy of message content, resilience to provider failure, and usability across both clients. These goals can conflict: richer logging may help diagnosis but expose sensitive text.", ["Correctness: preserve user input and do not silently send generated text.","Privacy: minimize transmitted content and avoid message-body logs.","Reliability: bound request time and recover the UI after errors.","Usability: explain loading, errors, and review responsibility."], "Prioritize controls using the deployment context and the sensitivity of the messages users may submit."),
("Input and output data dictionary", "The present request contains emailContent and tone. The web UI returns generatedReply to component state, while the backend response is a raw text value. A dictionary clarifies the role and handling expectations for each field.", ["emailContent: user-provided source message text; sensitive by context.","tone: optional text value; client options include Professional, Casual, and Friendly.","generated reply: model-produced draft; treat as unverified text.","error state: user-facing status; avoid embedding raw secrets or provider URLs."], "The current DTO does not express all proposed validation constraints; this dictionary is a design aid."),
("Use case: generate from the web form", "The normal web use case begins with the user opening the page, entering a message, selecting a tone, and requesting a reply. The output is shown for review and may be copied to another application.", ["Precondition: the web client and backend are available and provider settings are valid.","Main path: validate non-blank input, post the request, show progress, render returned text.","Alternate path: display a generic failure message when the request fails."], "Acceptance should verify both successful response handling and recovery from failures."),
("Use case: generate from Gmail", "The extension use case is intended to reduce context switching by placing an AI Reply control in a Gmail compose surface. It reads visible message text, calls the local API, and attempts to insert the result into the draft.", ["Precondition: extension is loaded and Gmail page selectors match.","Main path: locate compose controls, invoke generation, and place text in the editor.","Safety condition: the action must not trigger Gmail Send."], "The supplied image documents the visible action control, not a verified backend result or insertion outcome."),
("Requirement traceability worksheet", "The following worksheet structure can connect report claims to evidence. Keeping this trace helps future revisions avoid calling a planned behavior implemented.", ["Requirement ID and description.","Implementation location and observed source behavior.","Verification case, evidence artifact, result, and revision."], "A requirement without a test or source reference should remain an open item."),
("Non-functional constraints", "Several constraints follow from the architecture even though they are not fully implemented. Provider availability, network latency, browser DOM changes, and credential configuration all affect the user-visible result.", ["Generation depends on network access to the backend and Gemini service.","The extension depends on Gmail DOM structures that can change.","The local endpoint and fixed configuration are suitable only for development demonstration."], "Document each environment boundary before a public demonstration."),
("Out-of-scope capabilities", "The source review did not establish account management, message history storage, automatic email delivery, analytics, or attachment processing. Stating this boundary prevents readers from inferring capabilities from the product concept.", ["No automatic send action is described in the reviewed clients.","No application database or user-account workflow is identified in the supplied files.","The extension interacts with page markup rather than a Gmail API integration."], "Revisit scope only when corresponding implementation and verification evidence exist."),
("Assumptions and dependencies", "A local demonstration assumes that the correct runtimes are installed, the provider URL and key are configured, and the backend is reachable from the client. Gmail integration adds browser permission and page-structure assumptions.", ["React dependencies install successfully in the frontend directory.","Java and Maven can build and launch the backend.","Provider account, quota, and endpoint are valid for the configured model."], "Keep assumptions visible in release notes and setup instructions."),
("Risk register", "A compact risk register makes important technical and privacy risks actionable. Ratings should be revisited after implementation evidence is collected rather than treated as fixed facts.", ["R1: provider or network failure prevents generation; mitigate with bounded timeouts and recovery.","R2: key exposure through URL or logs; mitigate with secret handling and redaction.","R3: Gmail selector change breaks insertion; mitigate with browser regression checks.","R4: unsupported model claims; mitigate with user review and prompt constraints."], "Record owner, likelihood, impact, mitigation, and residual risk for each release."),
("Design decision record", "A decision record captures why the prototype uses a backend between browser clients and Gemini. This separation keeps provider credentials out of ordinary client code, but it does not by itself guarantee safe deployment or prevent exposure through request logs.", ["Decision: both client paths call a shared server endpoint.","Benefit: one place to construct prompts and manage provider communication.","Trade-off: backend availability and deployment configuration become required."], "Re-evaluate decisions when adding authentication, additional clients, or production hosting."),
]),
("H", "INTERACTION AND COMPONENT DESIGN", [
("Component responsibility matrix", "The interface, controller, service, and extension each perform distinct work. Clear ownership reduces duplicated validation and ambiguous error handling.", ["React App: collect fields, manage loading/error state, show and copy output.","Controller: bind request and delegate generation.","Gemini service: construct prompt, call provider, parse candidate text.","Content script: find Gmail page elements, request reply, attempt insertion."], "A future modular design can retain these boundaries while adding typed contracts."),
("Web request sequence", "The web sequence starts at the form and ends when the returned reply becomes visible. Error and loading transitions are part of the sequence and should be examined alongside the happy path.", ["User activates Generate Reply after entering content.","React posts a JSON request to the backend route.","Service sends a prompt to Gemini and parses provider output.","React renders the result or a user-facing error."], "Timing evidence should distinguish client, backend, and provider delays where instrumentation permits."),
("Gmail extension sequence", "The extension sequence begins with a page mutation that makes a compose toolbar available. A content script adds an action, reads message content, requests the service, and updates the compose editor.", ["Observe page changes and identify the intended compose container.","Create an accessible AI Reply action and prevent duplicate controls.","Capture only the active conversation context required for drafting.","Insert the result without sending or overwriting unrelated draft text."], "DOM interaction must be tested after Gmail UI changes and with multiple compose windows."),
("State model for the web form", "The web form can be reasoned about as idle, invalid, submitting, success, or error states. The React implementation has input, tone, generated reply, loading, and error state values; not all transitions are presented as a formal state machine.", ["Idle: no request is active.","Invalid: input is blank or whitespace-only.","Submitting: disable duplicate submission and show progress.","Success or error: show result or recovery guidance and restore controls."], "State transition tests help identify stale errors or old output left visible after a new request."),
("State model for the extension action", "The Gmail action should move from ready to generating and then to inserted or failed. In the current code, the button label and disabled state are managed around a request, but the actual interaction depends on selectors and editor behavior.", ["Ready: the correct message and compose surface are identified.","Generating: prevent duplicate clicks and signal progress.","Inserted: make the inserted text clear to the user.","Failed: restore the action and explain a safe retry path."], "Do not equate button presence with successful message extraction or insertion."),
("Sequence diagram notation guide", "A sequence diagram should show the user, selected client, backend controller, generation service, and Gemini endpoint as separate lifelines. The response should travel back through the same layers before it is displayed or inserted.", ["Label the JSON request fields and the provider prompt separately.","Show an alternate branch for provider error and parse failure.","Mark the point at which email content leaves the browser trust boundary."], "This diagram is a documentation artifact; it is not execution trace evidence."),
("Component interaction and trust boundaries", "The application has a browser-side trust area, a backend service area, and an external provider area. Gmail page content and model output are both untrusted inputs for different reasons.", ["Validate user input at the server boundary.","Keep provider credentials on the backend and redact them from diagnostics.","Treat generated text as data to review, not as an instruction to execute."], "Deployment diagrams should add the actual hosting, TLS, firewall, and logging components."),
("API endpoint specification", "The source route is POST /api/email/generate. The request object has emailContent and tone fields; the response currently is a raw text string. A stable API spec should define examples and errors before external clients depend on it.", ["Document content type, required fields, maximum sizes, and tone vocabulary.","Set explicit response examples for success and each failure category.","Define timeout behavior and whether repeated requests are idempotent."], "Treat examples as a proposed specification until adopted in code."),
("Prompt assembly specification", "The current service creates a professional email reply instruction, adds a tone instruction when supplied, and appends the original email. A more explicit contract can reduce conflicting tone directions and constrain unsupported claims.", ["Use one base instruction and one validated tone instruction.","Mark source email as quoted data, not trusted instructions.","Ask for concise text and no fabricated facts; preserve unknown details as placeholders."], "Evaluate prompt revisions using synthetic cases and record model/version conditions."),
("Provider response schema", "The service selects the first candidate and first text part from the provider JSON. A schema checklist can identify missing branches that should be handled before deployment.", ["Success: at least one candidate with a text part.","No candidate, empty content, or blocked response: controlled error.","HTTP error, malformed JSON, or timeout: stable client-visible failure."], "Provider response formats can change; validate against current documentation before updating the parser."),
("UI layout and accessibility map", "The React interface presents a title, multiline input, tone selector, submit action, progress indicator, generated text, and copy action. The visual order should match keyboard and screen-reader reading order.", ["Give every form control a programmatic label.","Announce progress and errors without moving focus unexpectedly.","Support zoom, narrow viewports, keyboard use, and visible focus."], "The supplied initial-state screenshot documents appearance only and is not an accessibility test."),
("Extension selector inventory", "The content script relies on selectors for the compose toolbar, message text, and editable compose box. A selector inventory records why each candidate is used and how failure is handled.", ["List current primary and fallback selectors with source evidence.","Test every fallback path; review loops that may return after the first miss.","Avoid silently choosing an unrelated message or compose surface."], "Do not depend on private or undocumented DOM structure without monitoring its fragility."),
]),
("I", "DETAILED VALIDATION AND EVIDENCE WORKSHEETS", [
("Test environment record", "Every executed validation case should identify the environment sufficiently to reproduce the result without recording sensitive email content. Redact credentials and personal identifiers from evidence.", ["Record source revision, operating system, Java, Node, browser, and dependency versions.","Record client path, backend URL category, provider/model configuration category, and network condition.","Use a synthetic message ID rather than storing the actual message body."], "A blank environment field should be treated as a reproducibility limitation."),
("Test case: blank input", "This case confirms that empty and whitespace-only input is stopped in the web client and rejected by the API if submitted directly. The repository currently shows a web-side check but no equivalent server validation annotations.", ["Prepare empty and whitespace-only values.","Attempt the normal UI flow and a direct API request.","Record client response, HTTP status, and whether a provider call occurred."], "Expected server-side rejection remains a proposed requirement until implemented."),
("Test case: tone behavior", "Tone behavior should be compared across Professional, Casual, Friendly, and no explicit tone. The current base prompt always requests professional wording, which can conflict with the optional tone instruction.", ["Use the same neutral synthetic message for each tone.","Record whether the result follows tone without changing factual details.","Compare the React selector with the extension's fixed professional value."], "Model outputs vary; record provider and model conditions and avoid claiming deterministic wording."),
("Test case: model response success", "The success case verifies that a valid provider response is parsed and relayed to both clients. A mock response can isolate parser behavior from network availability; a live request separately tests credentials and connectivity.", ["Use a controlled response containing a candidate and text part.","Confirm the API response is non-empty and the client displays it.","Check that newlines and punctuation are preserved."], "Keep mock and live evidence clearly distinguished."),
("Test case: malformed provider output", "Negative parser cases should include missing candidates, missing content, missing parts, empty text, invalid JSON, refusal content, and upstream error bodies. These cases target assumptions visible in the current first-candidate/first-part parser.", ["Prepare one fixture per malformed response shape.","Verify no unhandled exception or raw stack trace reaches the user.","Confirm the client receives a stable failure outcome."], "The current behavior is not asserted until these cases are run."),
("Test case: timeout and network loss", "A slow or disconnected provider can leave a request waiting and the client in a loading state. Test bounded request duration and guaranteed recovery for both clients.", ["Simulate connection refused, delayed response, and disconnect.","Measure elapsed time to a user-visible failure.","Confirm button state and controls recover after failure."], "Select timeouts based on acceptable user wait and provider behavior; do not add unbounded retries."),
("Test case: clipboard action", "The React copy action uses the browser clipboard API. Verify the result after user activation, then test denied permission and unavailable API behavior.", ["Copy a reply containing line breaks and punctuation.","Paste into a local text editor and compare exact content.","Observe whether failure or success is communicated clearly."], "The source does not currently expose a visible copy confirmation state."),
("Test case: Gmail action presence", "This case verifies that the extension action appears once in the intended Gmail compose surface and remains correctly associated with the active message.", ["Open a new compose, a reply, and multiple compose windows.","Observe control count after repeated DOM mutations.","Navigate between messages and confirm action scope."], "The supplied Work 13 image is visual evidence of a compose view with the AI Reply action, not this complete test."),
("Test case: Gmail extraction and insertion", "Verify that the extracted text matches the intended conversation context and the returned reply is inserted at the correct location in the draft. The extension must not trigger Send or overwrite unrelated text.", ["Use a synthetic message and a draft with existing text.","Compare extracted content with the intended message only.","Confirm inserted text placement and preserved draft content."], "Use a test mailbox and remove identifying information from saved evidence."),
("Test case: origin and credential handling", "Review browser assets, requests, source files, logs, and screenshots for credentials and sensitive content. Verify cross-origin behavior against the intended deployment origin.", ["Search the client build and extension files for provider keys.","Inspect request URLs and sanitized server logs.","Attempt an unapproved origin and record the observed policy."], "Source currently appends the key to the configured Gemini URL, so production logging behavior needs explicit review."),
("Defect severity and triage", "A consistent severity scheme helps the team prioritize issues without overstating prototype defects. Severity should reflect user impact, privacy, data loss, and availability.", ["Critical: secret exposure, unintended sending, or cross-user data disclosure.","High: core workflow fails or overwrites a user's draft.","Medium: recoverable failure or significant accessibility barrier.","Low: cosmetic issue with a clear workaround."], "Agree severity criteria before triage and record the rationale for each assignment."),
("Test evidence and sign-off form", "A sign-off form should make clear what was run, what was observed, and what remains open. Planned cases should never be counted as passing evidence.", ["Fields: case ID, revision, environment, observed result, status, evidence path, defect ID.","Reviewer confirms no secrets or private email data are included.","Approver records date and any accepted residual risk."], "Use 'not run' or 'blocked' where applicable and preserve the evidence needed for later review."),
]),
("J", "DEPLOYMENT, QUALITY, AND HANDOVER REFERENCE", [
("Local setup checklist", "A reproducible local setup should follow the actual repository layout and keep provider secrets out of checked-in files. The supplied README describes Node.js, Java, Maven, and Gemini configuration, but its sample paths should be checked against the repository.", ["Confirm frontend, backend, and extension directories.","Install dependencies and verify runtime versions.","Set local GEMINI_URL and GEMINI_KEY securely before startup."], "A clean setup should be repeated from a fresh checkout before handover."),
("Configuration matrix", "Configuration values should be separated by environment so a local URL cannot accidentally ship in a production client. The current React and extension sources use a local endpoint, and the extension also has a fixed tone.", ["Development: local API, test credentials, synthetic content.","Staging: protected provider secret, HTTPS, test mailbox.","Production: managed secret store, restrictive origin policy, monitored quotas."], "This matrix describes recommended environments, not existing deployment environments."),
("Release gate", "A release gate should require code, security, integration, and documentation evidence before the prototype is used with real messages. A gate also prevents screenshots or prose from implying a live feature that was not exercised.", ["Builds are reproducible and configuration is externalized.","API validation, failure mapping, and basic client recovery are verified.","Extension behavior is checked against the target browser and Gmail view.","Privacy notice and user review responsibility are clear."], "Record any waived item and its owner; do not silently call the release production-ready."),
("Backup and rollback plan", "A rollback plan helps restore a known version if a release changes prompt behavior, breaks Gmail selectors, or exposes a service issue. The current source does not contain a deployment pipeline, so these are handover recommendations.", ["Tag the source revision and preserve build artifacts.","Keep the previous known-good backend and extension package available.","Document how to disable the extension or revert the service configuration."], "Test rollback steps in a non-production environment before relying on them."),
("Operational ownership", "A live service needs a named owner for provider credentials, endpoint availability, browser compatibility, incident response, and privacy requests. No production ownership structure was supplied with the project files.", ["Assign a technical contact for API and deployment issues.","Define a process for credential rotation and quota review.","Provide a support route that does not ask users to send private email text."], "Record role names and contact details in the deployment-specific handover, not this generic report."),
("Privacy notice checklist", "Before sending real email content, users need a clear explanation of what is transmitted and to which provider. The source code alone cannot establish data retention terms, contractual controls, or hosting logs.", ["Explain that message text and selected tone reach the backend and model provider.","State applicable retention, access, and deletion practices from verified deployment terms.","Offer guidance to omit sensitive personal or confidential information."], "Have the notice reviewed against actual provider and institutional requirements."),
("Known limitations register", "The report should present current boundaries and future work together so that prototype behavior is not mistaken for a validated service. These items were observed by reviewing the supplied source.", ["Limited server-side input validation and provider error mapping.","Response parser assumes a first candidate and first text part.","Extension relies on Gmail DOM selectors and fixed configuration.","No supplied evidence of executed end-to-end tests or production deployment."], "Update this register when a limitation is fixed and verified."),
("Glossary and handover summary", "A concise glossary helps a reviewer understand the terminology used across the architecture and report. The handover summary records the component boundaries and next actions.", ["Client: React page or Gmail content script that begins a request.","Backend: Spring Boot API and Gemini service integration.","Draft: generated text presented for human review.","Handover: configuration, build, validation, and ownership materials."], "The next engineering priorities are request validation, response handling, secure configuration, and verified client integration."),
])]
for prefix, heading, records in extra_sections:
    for n, (topic, intro, points, review) in enumerate(records, 1):
        add(f"{prefix}.{n} {topic}", intro, points, review)

