# Appendix L adds eight pages of focused implementation and release analysis.
l_records = [
    ("L.1 Request contract and boundary validation", "The current request crosses two boundaries: a browser client submits JSON to Spring Boot, and the backend submits a prompt to Gemini. A reliable contract should make the checks at each boundary explicit instead of relying on the React form alone.", ["Require a non-null emailContent string and reject whitespace-only input on the server.", "Define an allow-list for tone and decide whether omission maps to a neutral default.", "Set a documented maximum request size before any provider call.", "Keep client-side checks for immediate feedback, but treat backend validation as authoritative."], "The checked-in DTO contains emailContent and tone without validation annotations. Add contract tests for accepted and rejected payloads before changing either client."),
    ("L.2 Success, failure, and response semantics", "The controller currently returns a raw string and the service can also return an error string after a parsing exception. That makes a generated reply and a service failure difficult for clients to distinguish. A stable contract should separate successful output from failure metadata.", ["Choose either a documented text response or a JSON object with a reply field; update both clients together.", "Map invalid requests, provider rejection, timeout, empty output, and unexpected parsing failures to distinct status categories.", "Return user-safe messages while keeping implementation details in protected diagnostics.", "Ensure the React and extension clients always restore their loading state."], "These are proposed contract improvements, not behaviors currently verified in the source. Preserve backward compatibility deliberately if an API response shape changes."),
    ("L.3 Prompt composition and tone precedence", "The service constructs a professional-reply instruction and appends a second instruction when a tone is supplied. Since Professional, Casual, and Friendly can conflict with the unconditional base instruction, prompt construction should have one clear source of tone authority.", ["Build a neutral base instruction and add exactly one validated tone directive.", "Keep the request's original message visibly separated from system guidance.", "Specify concise output and no invented subject line if that remains the product behavior.", "Use a neutral default when tone is absent and document it for both client paths."], "A deterministic prompt-builder unit test can verify the exact prompt without a live provider. Tone quality still needs model evaluation under recorded conditions."),
    ("L.4 Untrusted message content and output review", "An incoming email is user-provided data, not trusted instruction for the application. It may contain quoted text, signatures, or instructions directed at an AI system. The generated reply is also untrusted output because it can omit context or introduce unsupported details.", ["Delimit the source email and instruct the model to treat it as context rather than governing instructions.", "Do not let generated content trigger application actions or email sending.", "Keep the reply editable and visibly subject to review before use.", "Ask users to verify names, dates, amounts, links, commitments, and sensitive claims."], "Prompt safeguards reduce ambiguity but cannot guarantee accuracy. Human review remains a product requirement and should be stated at the point of use."),
    ("L.5 Gmail DOM lifecycle and mutation handling", "The extension uses a MutationObserver because Gmail can add and replace compose elements after the page initially loads. Observer callbacks can receive batches of changes, and repeated callbacks can cause duplicate actions unless insertion is idempotent.", ["Check each relevant compose container rather than assuming the first changed node is the target.", "Mark an injected control or use a scoped query to prevent duplicates.", "Disconnect or narrow observers when a compose surface is removed.", "Handle navigation, collapsed drafts, and newly opened compose windows explicitly."], "The supplied selector helpers return from their first loop iteration, so fallback behavior needs correction. Verify lifecycle cases in a test mailbox without risking unsaved drafts."),
    ("L.6 Compose scoping and draft preservation", "A Gmail page can contain several conversations and compose windows at once. Reading the first matching message or inserting into the first editable textbox can associate a reply with the wrong context. The action should be scoped from the button's own compose or conversation container.", ["Resolve the active conversation from a stable ancestor before extracting message text.", "Pair the AI Reply control with the editor in the same compose surface.", "Preserve existing draft content and define whether insertion replaces a selection or appends text.", "Never locate or activate the Send control as part of generation."], "These rules address data association and accidental overwrite risk. The current screenshot proves button placement only; it does not prove these behaviors."),
    ("L.7 React request state and recovery", "The React component stores content, tone, result, loading, and error independently. Independent state is simple for a prototype but can allow stale output or error text to remain visible across successive requests if transition rules are incomplete.", ["On a new request, clear or label the previous result so it is not mistaken for the new reply.", "Set loading before the request and reset it in a finally path for every outcome.", "Define whether edits to input invalidate the displayed result.", "Prevent duplicate submissions while a request is active and preserve the user's entered text after failure."], "A small state-transition table should guide UI tests for idle, invalid, submitting, success, and error states. Do not claim cancellation or retries until they exist in code."),
    ("L.8 Accessible feedback and keyboard operation", "The web form uses Material UI controls, while the Gmail action is created directly in the page. A visually clear action can still be difficult to use if it lacks a programmatic name, keyboard activation, visible focus, or announced status changes.", ["Use semantic buttons and labels for both clients.", "Expose loading and error changes to assistive technology without repeatedly interrupting the user.", "Check contrast, zoom, focus order, and operation without a pointer.", "Keep the inserted reply editable and provide a clear recovery path if insertion fails."], "Accessibility checks must be performed in the running interface. Component-library usage alone does not establish conformance."),
    ("L.9 Data inventory and trust boundaries", "The principal user content is emailContent, accompanied by a tone value. The web client sends this information to the backend, which includes the email text in a provider prompt. The model reply travels back through the service to the client and may then be copied or placed in a Gmail draft.", ["Identify email text and generated text as sensitive by context, even when no account data is stored locally.", "Document each transfer, recipient, transport, and storage point for the deployment actually used.", "Minimize diagnostic collection and avoid retaining message bodies by default.", "Treat browser page content and model output as untrusted at their respective boundaries."], "No database or persistent reply-history subsystem was identified in the supplied source, but deployment logs and provider processing still require review."),
    ("L.10 Credential protection, logs, and privacy notice", "The Gemini key is configured on the backend but appended to the provider URL in the current service. URLs can appear in diagnostics or monitoring systems, so secret placement and log redaction need explicit attention. A user notice should describe the actual message-processing path.", ["Use the provider's supported authentication mechanism and protect secrets outside the repository.", "Redact query parameters and request bodies from logs, traces, and error reports.", "Restrict CORS and extension host access to the deployment's required origins.", "Explain that email text is sent to the backend and external model provider, using verified retention terms."], "A source-level review cannot establish provider retention, contractual protections, or production logging behavior. Verify those facts in the target environment."),
    ("L.11 Environment configuration and deployment separation", "The React and extension sources currently use a local HTTP endpoint, while the backend reads provider values from environment placeholders. Local demonstration, staging, and public deployment need separate configuration so that development URLs and secrets cannot be carried into a release accidentally.", ["Make the API base URL environment-specific in both clients.", "Use HTTPS outside local development and a protected secret store for provider credentials.", "Set explicit origins, request size limits, and timeouts at the service boundary.", "Provide example configuration with placeholders rather than real keys."], "The source does not show a production deployment. Treat deployment profiles and operational ownership as work to complete, not as existing capabilities."),
    ("L.12 Availability, latency, and resource behavior", "A generation request depends on the browser, backend, network, provider availability, and provider quota. The service calls a reactive client and then blocks for a result, so request duration and thread usage should be measured under realistic conditions before selecting deployment capacity.", ["Set connect and response timeouts and return a controlled failure when they expire.", "Use bounded concurrency and request-size controls to protect the service.", "Avoid automatic retries that can create duplicate provider cost without a clear policy.", "Measure client wait time and backend/provider time separately using synthetic requests."], "No latency or load measurements were supplied. Capacity and performance claims should remain open until an instrumented test records them."),
    ("L.13 Test architecture and isolation", "Validation can be split into deterministic component checks and environment-dependent integration checks. Prompt construction and parsing can be tested with fixtures; network credentials, provider availability, browser DOM behavior, and user interaction require separate evidence.", ["Unit-test request validation, tone selection, prompt assembly, and provider-response parsing.", "Controller-test status codes and response shape without external network access.", "Use a mock provider for reproducible client-to-service cases.", "Run live-provider and Gmail checks only with synthetic or consented content."], "Keep test results tied to a source revision and identify which layers were mocked. A passing unit test is not evidence of Gmail insertion or provider availability."),
    ("L.14 Test data and evidence integrity", "Email examples can contain personal, business, or authentication data. The project needs a test-data policy that allows useful workflow checks without placing real mailbox content in reports, logs, or screenshots.", ["Use short synthetic messages representing acknowledgement, scheduling, clarification, and update tasks.", "Do not include real names, email addresses, credentials, financial details, or confidential attachments.", "Capture only the interface state needed to demonstrate the workflow.", "Record the environment, case ID, observed result, and evidence path without retaining the full message body."], "The two included images show the initial web state and Gmail action placement. They do not document a live model response or a measured test result."),
    ("L.15 Release acceptance and residual risk", "A release decision should be based on evidence for the complete request path and on explicit treatment of the remaining privacy, reliability, and Gmail-integration risks. A local prototype can be suitable for a controlled demonstration while still being unsuitable for real confidential mail.", ["Require validated input, stable error outcomes, secure configuration, and recoverable client states.", "Verify Gmail control placement, correct message scoping, draft preservation, and absence of send activation.", "Review the notice and provider-processing terms for the target deployment.", "Record unresolved issues, owners, and any demonstration restrictions."], "Do not label the system production-ready based on source inspection or screenshots alone. Revisit acceptance when fixes and test evidence are available."),
    ("L.16 Maintenance and change traceability", "Changes to provider models, prompts, Gmail selectors, dependency versions, and API schemas can alter behavior even when the interface looks unchanged. A maintainable handover should connect each release artifact to the source revision and to the evidence used for acceptance.", ["Record commit, build date, runtime versions, configuration category, and package outputs.", "Version prompt and API changes with focused regression cases.", "Review extension selector compatibility and provider response assumptions when upstream formats change.", "Preserve rollback information and remove obsolete build artifacts only after handover."], "No formal release or change log was supplied. Introduce traceability as a future control and keep report claims aligned with the exact revision reviewed."),
]
for title, intro, points, review in l_records:
    add(title, intro, points, review)
