add_bottom_section(9,"1.6 Scope boundary and operating assumptions",
    "The prototype accepts text supplied by a user and returns candidate wording for review. The documented scope includes a React web client, a Spring Boot service, a Gemini provider call, and a Gmail-oriented content script. It does not include automatic sending, account management, saved reply history, analytics, attachment processing, or a verified production deployment. Local operation assumes the required runtimes are installed, provider configuration is available to the backend, and both clients can reach the same API. These assumptions should be checked during setup and recorded with any demonstration. Real email content should not be used until the deployment's provider processing, transport, logging, and notice have been reviewed.",64,194)
add_bottom_section(16,"3.7 Design decisions and implementation boundary",
    "A shared backend keeps provider communication in one service and avoids placing the Gemini credential in ordinary client code. The trade-off is that both the standalone page and the Gmail action depend on the API being reachable and on the service translating provider outcomes consistently. The web client is a separate Vite application; the Gmail integration is a Manifest V3 content script that operates on rendered page elements. This separation explains why the two paths offer different tone controls and have different failure modes. The current implementation is best treated as a local prototype: its endpoints, CORS policy, credential transport, request validation, and Gmail selectors need further work before broader deployment. The report describes source behavior separately from these recommended changes. Component boundaries should guide verification: prompt tests belong at the backend service, visual and clipboard checks belong in the React client, and selector and editor checks require a browser. A passing result in one layer should not be treated as proof for the others. Keep each check linked to the reviewed source revision. The deployment package should also identify which API address the extension expects and which origins the service accepts. Review those values after every environment change so that a development configuration is not accidentally mistaken for a production one.",64,308)
add_bottom_section(18,"4.1.4 Acceptance criteria and evidence handling",
    "A validation result should identify the source revision, environment, test case, observed behavior, and evidence reference. Mark each case as passed, failed, blocked, or not run; do not infer a pass from source inspection or from a screenshot. Use synthetic or consented messages, redact account details, and avoid storing email text in logs or worksheets. Acceptance for a controlled demonstration should cover a valid request, invalid input, provider failure, client recovery, and the visible Gmail action. Any claim of successful insertion requires a browser observation that confirms the intended compose editor changed while existing draft text remained intact and the Send action was not activated. Production acceptance also requires security, privacy, and accessibility review.",64,216)

